            <div xml:id="d64">

                <pb facs="0194" n="95" sameAs="frus:frus1936v02/pg95"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.5131/1646: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Bullitt</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-11-03T15:00:00-05:00">November 3, 1936—3
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>443. We are much interested in the French proposal to conduct informal
                    conversations along the lines reported in your 1067 of October <pb facs="0195"
                        n="96" xml:id="pg96"/> 30, 10 p.m. Action tending towards a general
                    reduction of restrictions to international trade along the broad lines advocated
                    by this Government would be most welcome. Before considering sending officials
                    from here we would want to know something more concrete concerning the scope and
                    purposes of the proposed conversations. Have the British been similarly
                    approached and if so what was their attitude? Feis not available as already
                    announced that he is sailing November 7th. with the American delegation to the
                    Conference of American Republics at Buenos Aires.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
